/** @mainpage Qore imagemagick Module

    @tableofcontents

    @section imagemagickintro imagemagick Module Introduction

    <img src="imagemagick-logo.svg" width="96" alt="imagemagick module logo"/>

    The \c imagemagick module provides comprehensive image processing and manipulation capabilities
    for %Qore by wrapping the ImageMagick MagickWand C API. It supports over 200 image formats and
    offers a wide range of operations including:

    - Image I/O: read and write images from files or in-memory binary data
    - Format conversion: convert between PNG, JPEG, GIF, BMP, TIFF, WebP, and many other formats
    - Resizing: multiple algorithms including Lanczos, Mitchell, and bilinear filtering
    - Cropping, trimming, and canvas manipulation
    - Transformations: rotation, flipping, shearing, deskewing, auto-orientation
    - Blur and sharpen: Gaussian, adaptive, unsharp mask, motion blur, and more
    - Color adjustments: brightness, contrast, gamma, levels, modulate, normalize
    - Artistic effects: charcoal, sepia, oil paint, edge detection, emboss, and more
    - Compositing: overlay images with various blending operators
    - Drawing: vector shapes, text annotations, and custom drawing operations
    - Metadata: read/write EXIF, ICC profiles, and image properties
    - Multi-image support: animated GIF frames, multi-page TIFF

    To use this module, use the \c %%requires directive as follows:
    @code{.py}
%modern
%requires imagemagick
    @endcode

    This module provides the following classes:
    - @ref Qore::ImageMagick::MagickImage "MagickImage": the main image class for loading,
      manipulating, and saving images
    - @ref Qore::ImageMagick::MagickDrawing "MagickDrawing": vector graphics drawing class for
      shapes and text

    And the following hashdecls:
    - @ref Qore::ImageMagick::ImageInfo "ImageInfo": image metadata
    - @ref Qore::ImageMagick::ImageResolutionInfo "ImageResolutionInfo": image resolution (DPI)
    - @ref Qore::ImageMagick::PointInfo "PointInfo": x/y coordinate pair

    @section imagemagickcallerpaths Paths and Restricted Code

    The methods that take a file path, a font that names a file (@ref Qore::ImageMagick::MagickImage::annotate()
    "MagickImage::annotate()", @ref Qore::ImageMagick::MagickDrawing::setFont() "MagickDrawing::setFont()") and image
    data in a format whose data can name other files refuse it with \c PERMISSION-DENIED before anything is read or
    written if the code that gives it is restricted from the \c FILESYSTEM functional domain (for example with
    \c PO_NO_FILESYSTEM; for such formats, also from the \c NETWORK functional domain).  The methods with the
    \c FILESYSTEM functional domain cannot be called by such code at all; a method of an object of a module class that
    the host created and gave to restricted code, however, runs with the file system lent, so the paths, fonts and
    data that such a method gives these classes are decided with the code that the object was given to (with a %Qore
    that provides \c qore_caller_lent_to_restricts_domain(), which defines \c QORE_HAVE_CALLER_AMBIENT_PREDICATES;
    built with an older %Qore, only the calling code decides, and only for fonts and formats;
    @ref Qore::ImageMagick::IM_LENT_PATH_CHECKS tells which).  The file-system policy of a sandbox that governs the
    calling code applies when a file is opened (\c FILESYSTEM-ACCESS-DENIED).  Images given as data in other formats,
    registered font names and the state of an existing object can be used by restricted code.

    The code that decides is that of the caller of the module code that calls these classes, not the module's own
    %Program: a user module that restricted code calls (with or without a lent object) is refused the paths that it
    gives these classes for that code, also paths of its own (such as bundled images); module code that serves
    restricted code uses the methods that take and return data.

